Description:

The IMAP THREAD command now returns NO rather than corrupting memory when the
messages to be threaded would overflow the thread node count.  Previously, a
message with an excessive number of References could cause an out of bounds
write during threading.

Threading now also reads at most 20 references from any one message.  When a
message carries more than that, threading uses the root id, the parent id, and
the ids of the 18 most-recent predecessors of the parent -- following the same
advice given to clients about paring down References.  This cap also bounds
how much memory a single THREAD command can allocate; previously a mailbox of
messages carrying long References headers could drive that into the hundreds
of megabytes without any integer overflow occurring.

Tracked as GitHub security advisory GHSA-rhx5-p47p-5h9q.


